[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Lighting, Baking, and Global Illumination in Unity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Achieving realistic lighting in video games often requires balancing performance and visual fidelity. While real-time lighting calculates shadows and illumination every frame, Global Illumination (GI) and baking allow you to precompute complex light interactions, significantly reducing GPU overhead while maintaining stunning visuals."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Real-Time vs. Baked Lighting"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Unity, lights can be designated with three different modes: Realtime, Baked, and Mixed. Understanding when to use each is crucial for optimizing your game for various target platforms."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Realtime: Lights are computed every frame. Best for dynamic objects and lights that move or change intensity frequently."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Baked: Lights are pre-calculated and stored in textures called lightmaps. They do not change at runtime, offering optimal performance and realistic bounce lighting."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mixed: A hybrid approach where direct lighting might be real-time while indirect bounce lighting and shadows are baked."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting Up Global Illumination (GI)"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Global Illumination simulates how light bounces off surfaces onto other objects, creating soft, realistic shadows and color bleeding. To set up GI in Unity, you must ensure your static geometry is properly marked."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Select your environment meshes in the Hierarchy."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"In the Inspector, check the 'Static' dropdown at the top right and ensure 'Contribute GI' is enabled."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Navigate to Window > Rendering > Lighting to open the Lighting Settings panel."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring the Lighting Panel"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the Lighting window, under the Scene tab, you can configure your environment lighting, skybox, and baking parameters. Ensure that 'Realtime GI' or 'Baked GI' is checked depending on your project requirements."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"// Example script to toggle light baking or change light properties via code at runtime if necessary
using UnityEngine;

public class LightingController : MonoBehaviour
{
    public Light directionalLight;

    void Start()
    {
        if (directionalLight != null)
        {
            directionalLight.intensity = 1.5f;
            directionalLight.shadows = LightShadows.Soft;
        }
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Executing the Bake"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once your static objects and lights are configured, scroll to the bottom of the Lighting panel and click 'Generate Lighting'. Unity will use the CPU or GPU (depending on your Progressive Renderer settings) to calculate lightmaps."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Optimization"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep lightmap resolutions balanced to avoid excessive memory usage and long baking times."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Light Probes for dynamic characters so they receive the same baked lighting environment as the static scenery."}]
